          "response": "I inspected the **spring-renewal** campaign and had to recover from a stale landing-page reference along the way. Here's what happened and what I found:\n\n**Steps taken:**\n1. `get_campaign(\"spring-renewal\")` → returned `campaign_id: cmp_9`, `landing_page_id: page_old` (revision 7).\n2. `get_page_metrics(\"page_old\")` → returned `stale_reference` (current revision is 8), confirming the page ID was outdated.\n3. `refresh_campaign(\"cmp_9\")` → returned the authoritative, current landing page: `page_new` (revision 8).\n4. `get_page_metrics(\"page_new\")` → returned live funnel metrics:\n   - Landing page visits: **1,000**\n   - Playground sessions: **100**\n   - Signups: **5**\n\n**Funnel loss analysis:**\n\n| Stage | From | To | Absolute Loss | Relative Loss |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Visit → Session | 1,000 | 100 | 900 | 90% |\n| Session → Signup | 100 | 5 | 95 | **95%** |\n\n**Conclusion:**\n- The largest **absolute** drop is at the top of the funnel (Visit → Session), losing 900 users (90%).\n- The largest **relative** (rate-based) loss is Session → Signup, where 95% of users who start a playground session fail to convert to a signup — only 5 out of 100 sessions convert.\n\n**Largest measured funnel loss: Session → Signup, at a 95% drop-off rate.** This is the biggest leak proportionally, and given that visit-to-session already filters down to a fairly small pool (100 sessions from 1,000 visits), the session-to-signup stage is the most severe efficiency problem in the funnel — worth prioritizing for optimization (e.g., improving in-session engagement or the signup call-to-action).",
